    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d2">

                <pb facs="0018" n="4" sameAs="frus:frus1948v05p1/pg4"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890F.6363/1–2648</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum of Conversation, by Mr. Herbert A. Breakey of
                        the Petroleum Division</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1948-01-28">January 28, 1948</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <table>
                    <row role="label">
                        <cell rendition="#top #nowrap">Participants:</cell>
                        <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Messrs. [James] Terry Duce and [Philip C.]
                            Kidd, Arabian American Oil Company</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Colonel [William A.] Eddy, Arabian
                            American Oil Company</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Mr. Sanger, NE </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Mr. Jenkins, NE </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell rendition="#hanging-indent">Mr. Breakey, PED </cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <p>Mr. Terry Duce, of the Arabian American Oil Company, being in the city for a few
                    days, was invited to meet with some of the State Department officials to inform
                    them of the last developments regarding the Trans-Arabian pipe line.</p>
                <p>[Here follow four paragraphs of observations by Mr. Duce.]</p>
                <p>Mr. Duce summarized the situation as follows: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>The company is going ahead with the pipe line as planned.<anchor n="2"
                                xml:id="d2-f1"/>
                        </item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>This winter it is proceeding with the line in the south.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>The company is not at the present time interested in an alternative
                            route, but it may investigate such possibilities.</item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item>The company, however, will consult with the governments at present
                            concerned before attempting any alternative routes.</item>
                        <label>5.</label>
                        <item>If war develops in Palestine,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d2-f2"/> new plans
                            will have to be considered.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d2-f1" n="2" xml:id="d2-f1-content">
                <p>Cairo, on January 26, had advised that Aramco was giving consideration to
                    abandoning the Tapline project for six years and to negotiating transit rights
                    in Egypt (telegram 75, 883.6363/1–2648).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d2-f2" n="3" xml:id="d2-f2-content">
                <p>Documentation on Palestine is scheduled for publication in part 2 of the present
                    volume.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
